1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,581
You just broke something in Git.

2
00:00:02,581 --> 00:00:03,297
A bad edit,

3
00:00:03,297 --> 00:00:04,272
a wrong commit,

4
00:00:04,272 --> 00:00:05,508
a reset you regret.

5
00:00:05,508 --> 00:00:07,199
Most beginners panic here.

6
00:00:07,749 --> 00:00:10,437
Today you learn the undo toolbox: restore,

7
00:00:10,437 --> 00:00:10,885
revert,

8
00:00:10,885 --> 00:00:11,525
and reset,

9
00:00:11,525 --> 00:00:13,573
and how to choose the right one.

10
00:00:14,123 --> 00:00:18,005
And the safety net that almost never lets you lose work: the ref log.

11
00:00:18,555 --> 00:00:21,371
As always, there is a lab with a checker at the end.

12
00:00:23,421 --> 00:00:25,591
Git keeps your work in three places.

13
00:00:25,591 --> 00:00:26,736
The working folder,

14
00:00:26,736 --> 00:00:27,640
where you edit.

15
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:28,664
The staging area,

16
00:00:28,664 --> 00:00:30,713
where you prepare the next commit.

17
00:00:30,713 --> 00:00:31,678
And the history,

18
00:00:31,678 --> 00:00:33,064
the commits themselves.

19
00:00:33,614 --> 00:00:38,840
Each undo command works on one of these places. First question: where is the mistake?

20
00:00:39,390 --> 00:00:44,404
A mistake in a file you have not committed? Use git restore. It puts the file back.

21
00:00:44,954 --> 00:00:48,649
A mistake in a commit that is already shared with your team?

22
00:00:48,649 --> 00:00:49,573
Use git revert.

23
00:00:49,573 --> 00:00:52,407
It adds a new commit that cancels the old one.

24
00:00:52,407 --> 00:00:53,700
History stays honest.

25
00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:57,429
A mistake in commits that are only on your computer?

26
00:00:57,429 --> 00:00:58,284
Use git reset.

27
00:00:58,284 --> 00:00:59,874
It moves your branch back,

28
00:00:59,874 --> 00:01:01,952
and rewrites your private history.

29
00:01:02,502 --> 00:01:06,726
And if you ever lose a commit, the ref log remembers where your branch has been.

30
00:01:08,776 --> 00:01:13,939
Let us see it live. A small notes site, three commits. Go in. Type: CD, site.

31
00:01:14,489 --> 00:01:17,753
Make a mistake: add a junk line to the page.

32
00:01:17,753 --> 00:01:18,569
Type: echo,

33
00:01:18,569 --> 00:01:19,014
quote,

34
00:01:19,014 --> 00:01:20,127
paragraph oops,

35
00:01:20,127 --> 00:01:20,572
quote,

36
00:01:20,572 --> 00:01:21,388
two arrows,

37
00:01:21,388 --> 00:01:22,723
index dot H T M L.

38
00:01:23,273 --> 00:01:26,431
Check the status. Type: git status, dash S.

39
00:01:26,981 --> 00:01:29,064
An M in the second column: modified,

40
00:01:29,064 --> 00:01:29,701
not staged.

41
00:01:29,701 --> 00:01:31,380
Git tells you how to undo it,

42
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:32,769
but here is the command.

43
00:01:32,769 --> 00:01:33,811
Type: git restore,

44
00:01:33,811 --> 00:01:34,853
index dot H T M L.

45
00:01:35,403 --> 00:01:40,565
Nothing printed, which is good. Check the status again. Type: git status, dash S.

46
00:01:41,115 --> 00:01:41,581
Clean.

47
00:01:41,581 --> 00:01:43,209
Now a second mistake,

48
00:01:43,209 --> 00:01:44,528
staged this time.

49
00:01:44,528 --> 00:01:45,847
Add a draft line.

50
00:01:45,847 --> 00:01:46,700
Type: echo,

51
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:47,165
quote,

52
00:01:47,165 --> 00:01:48,406
paragraph draft,

53
00:01:48,406 --> 00:01:48,871
quote,

54
00:01:48,871 --> 00:01:49,725
two arrows,

55
00:01:49,725 --> 00:01:51,121
index dot H T M L.

56
00:01:51,671 --> 00:01:55,063
Stage it. Type: git add, index dot H T M L.

57
00:01:55,613 --> 00:01:58,161
To take it out of the staging area,

58
00:01:58,161 --> 00:02:00,564
use restore with the staged flag.

59
00:02:00,564 --> 00:02:01,875
Type: git restore,

60
00:02:01,875 --> 00:02:03,113
dash dash staged,

61
00:02:03,113 --> 00:02:04,423
index dot H T M L.

62
00:02:04,973 --> 00:02:08,045
Status again. Type: git status, dash S.

63
00:02:08,595 --> 00:02:10,978
Now the M is in the second column again.

64
00:02:10,978 --> 00:02:12,944
The change left the staging area,

65
00:02:12,944 --> 00:02:15,029
but your edit is still in the file.

66
00:02:15,029 --> 00:02:15,923
Discard it too.

67
00:02:15,923 --> 00:02:16,995
Type: git restore,

68
00:02:16,995 --> 00:02:18,067
index dot H T M L.

69
00:02:18,617 --> 00:02:22,434
One more power: restore a file from an older commit.

70
00:02:22,434 --> 00:02:23,756
Type: git restore,

71
00:02:23,756 --> 00:02:26,619
dash dash source equals head tilde two,

72
00:02:26,619 --> 00:02:27,940
index dot H T M L.

73
00:02:28,490 --> 00:02:33,162
The file now comes from two commits ago. See the difference. Type: git diff.

74
00:02:33,712 --> 00:02:36,927
Careful: restore on a working file cannot be undone.

75
00:02:36,927 --> 00:02:38,968
Those edits were never committed,

76
00:02:38,968 --> 00:02:41,008
so Git has nothing to bring back.

77
00:02:43,058 --> 00:02:47,581
Now a bad commit that your team already has. Go in. Type: CD, site.

78
00:02:48,131 --> 00:02:51,885
Look at the history. Type: git log, dash dash one line.

79
00:02:52,435 --> 00:02:53,451
The last commit,

80
00:02:53,451 --> 00:02:54,847
Delete the ports note,

81
00:02:54,847 --> 00:02:55,736
was a mistake,

82
00:02:55,736 --> 00:02:57,323
and it is already shared.

83
00:02:57,323 --> 00:02:59,227
Do not rewrite shared history.

84
00:02:59,227 --> 00:02:59,862
Revert it.

85
00:02:59,862 --> 00:03:00,941
Type: git revert,

86
00:03:00,941 --> 00:03:02,401
dash dash no dash edit,

87
00:03:02,401 --> 00:03:02,718
head.

88
00:03:03,268 --> 00:03:07,300
Look at the history again. Type: git log, dash dash one line.

89
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:09,513
The bad commit is still there,

90
00:03:09,513 --> 00:03:11,065
and a new commit cancels it.

91
00:03:11,065 --> 00:03:12,784
Everyone can see what happened,

92
00:03:12,784 --> 00:03:14,170
and nobody's copy breaks.

93
00:03:14,170 --> 00:03:15,002
Check the file.

94
00:03:15,002 --> 00:03:16,554
Type: cat index dot H T M L.

95
00:03:17,104 --> 00:03:21,157
The ports line is back. Revert is the safe undo for shared history.

96
00:03:23,207 --> 00:03:27,794
Now commits that are only on your computer. Go in. Type: CD, site.

97
00:03:28,344 --> 00:03:32,098
Look at the history. Type: git log, dash dash one line.

98
00:03:32,648 --> 00:03:33,613
Two experiments,

99
00:03:33,613 --> 00:03:34,578
try a red title,

100
00:03:34,578 --> 00:03:35,844
and try a blue title.

101
00:03:35,844 --> 00:03:37,834
You want to undo the last commit,

102
00:03:37,834 --> 00:03:38,920
but keep the work.

103
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:39,885
Type: git reset,

104
00:03:39,885 --> 00:03:40,789
dash dash soft,

105
00:03:40,789 --> 00:03:41,694
head tilde one.

106
00:03:42,244 --> 00:03:45,401
Check the status. Type: git status, dash S.

107
00:03:45,951 --> 00:03:47,019
The commit is gone,

108
00:03:47,019 --> 00:03:48,761
but the change is still staged,

109
00:03:48,761 --> 00:03:50,447
with an M in the first column.

110
00:03:50,447 --> 00:03:52,021
Soft keeps the staging area.

111
00:03:52,021 --> 00:03:53,201
Now the default mode.

112
00:03:53,201 --> 00:03:54,100
Type: git reset.

113
00:03:54,650 --> 00:03:57,722
Status again. Type: git status, dash S.

114
00:03:58,272 --> 00:03:59,462
Now unstaged too.

115
00:03:59,462 --> 00:04:01,842
Mixed keeps your edit in the file,

116
00:04:01,842 --> 00:04:02,191
only.

117
00:04:02,191 --> 00:04:04,711
The third mode is the dangerous one.

118
00:04:04,711 --> 00:04:06,530
Hard throws the work away.

119
00:04:06,530 --> 00:04:07,650
Type: git reset,

120
00:04:07,650 --> 00:04:08,700
dash dash hard,

121
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:09,750
head tilde one.

122
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:16,017
Head is now at the older commit. Look at the history. Type: git log, dash dash one line.

123
00:04:16,567 --> 00:04:18,314
Soft keeps staged changes.

124
00:04:18,314 --> 00:04:19,523
Mixed keeps files.

125
00:04:19,523 --> 00:04:20,799
Hard keeps nothing.

126
00:04:20,799 --> 00:04:24,226
And never reset commits that your team already has.

127
00:04:26,376 --> 00:04:31,859
Now the safety net. Same repository, five commits. Go in. Type: CD, site.

128
00:04:32,409 --> 00:04:33,135
Imagine a slip.

129
00:04:33,135 --> 00:04:34,685
You meant to go back one commit,

130
00:04:34,685 --> 00:04:36,623
and you went back two with a hard reset.

131
00:04:36,623 --> 00:04:37,398
Type: git reset,

132
00:04:37,398 --> 00:04:38,125
dash dash hard,

133
00:04:38,125 --> 00:04:38,851
head tilde two.

134
00:04:39,401 --> 00:04:44,372
Two commits vanished. Check the history. Type: git log, dash dash one line.

135
00:04:44,922 --> 00:04:50,149
Do not panic. Git keeps a diary of where your head has been. Type: git reflog.

136
00:04:50,699 --> 00:04:51,988
Each line is a move.

137
00:04:51,988 --> 00:04:55,342
Head at one is where you were just before the reset.

138
00:04:55,342 --> 00:04:56,374
Jump back to it.

139
00:04:56,374 --> 00:04:57,405
Type: git reset,

140
00:04:57,405 --> 00:04:58,373
dash dash hard,

141
00:04:58,373 --> 00:04:59,147
head at one.

142
00:04:59,697 --> 00:05:03,921
Recovered. Check the history. Type: git log, dash dash one line.

143
00:05:04,471 --> 00:05:06,138
All five commits are back.

144
00:05:06,138 --> 00:05:07,484
The ref log is local,

145
00:05:07,484 --> 00:05:10,242
and it keeps entries for about ninety days,

146
00:05:10,242 --> 00:05:11,460
so recover quickly.

147
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:13,255
It only knows about commits,

148
00:05:13,255 --> 00:05:15,628
so uncommitted edits are not covered.

149
00:05:17,778 --> 00:05:20,551
Your task, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

150
00:05:21,101 --> 00:05:23,652
The lab is lab sixty-three dot S H,

151
00:05:23,652 --> 00:05:27,370
in the academy slash lab folder of git hub dot com,

152
00:05:27,370 --> 00:05:28,828
slash it dash waves,

153
00:05:28,828 --> 00:05:30,504
slash dev ops dash A I.

154
00:05:30,504 --> 00:05:32,180
Install it like before,

155
00:05:32,180 --> 00:05:33,347
then run lab up.

156
00:05:33,897 --> 00:05:37,967
It builds a repository with a messy situation: junk in a file,

157
00:05:37,967 --> 00:05:40,331
a bad commit that is already shared,

158
00:05:40,331 --> 00:05:42,366
and a commit that someone lost.

159
00:05:42,916 --> 00:05:47,887
One: discard the junk edit in start dot S H, and leave the working folder clean.

160
00:05:48,437 --> 00:05:53,109
Two: find the lost commit, Add retry setting, in the ref log, and bring it back.

161
00:05:53,659 --> 00:05:58,224
Three: revert the shared commit, Add debug flag. Do not rewrite history.

162
00:05:58,774 --> 00:06:03,403
Four: in answers dot text, write one number: how many commits are on main now.

163
00:06:03,953 --> 00:06:08,305
Then run lab check. Four pass lines. Restore, revert, reflog.

164
00:06:10,355 --> 00:06:12,339
Six points, and you need all six.

165
00:06:12,889 --> 00:06:14,724
The checker shows four pass lines.

166
00:06:15,274 --> 00:06:18,196
You reverted the shared commit instead of resetting it.

167
00:06:18,746 --> 00:06:21,477
The lost commit is back, found through the ref log.

168
00:06:22,027 --> 00:06:25,312
You can say when to use restore, revert, and reset.

169
00:06:25,862 --> 00:06:28,188
The number in answers dot text is correct.

170
00:06:28,738 --> 00:06:30,380
You ran lab down to clean up.

171
00:06:30,930 --> 00:06:32,552
To pass, you need all six.

172
00:06:34,602 --> 00:06:37,439
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

173
00:06:37,989 --> 00:06:40,208
One: install the lab, and run lab up.

174
00:06:40,758 --> 00:06:42,486
Two: restore the junk file.

175
00:06:43,036 --> 00:06:46,492
Three: find the lost commit in the ref log, and recover it.

176
00:06:47,042 --> 00:06:49,346
Four: revert the debug flag commit.

177
00:06:49,896 --> 00:06:53,267
Five: count the commits, write the number, and run lab check.

178
00:06:53,817 --> 00:06:55,289
Six: run lab down.

179
00:06:55,839 --> 00:06:57,673
Pause the video, and begin now.

180
00:07:13,223 --> 00:07:14,098
Quick check.

181
00:07:14,648 --> 00:07:17,421
Which command undoes an edit that is not committed?

182
00:07:17,971 --> 00:07:20,766
Why revert, and not reset, for a shared commit?

183
00:07:21,316 --> 00:07:24,153
What is the difference between soft, mixed, and hard?

184
00:07:24,703 --> 00:07:27,263
How do you recover after a bad hard reset?

185
00:07:27,813 --> 00:07:30,481
Objective covered: M C zero seven,

186
00:07:30,481 --> 00:07:31,736
objective seven,

187
00:07:31,736 --> 00:07:32,364
restore,

188
00:07:32,364 --> 00:07:32,913
revert,

189
00:07:32,913 --> 00:07:33,384
reset,

190
00:07:33,384 --> 00:07:34,640
and the ref log.

191
00:07:35,190 --> 00:07:37,259
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

192
00:07:47,809 --> 00:07:51,308
Next: secrets, credentials, and repository access.

193
00:07:51,858 --> 00:07:55,186
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
